.TH BOOTO-CHECKS 7 2026-11-09 "Booto" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
booto-checks \- every finding Booto can report, what it means, and what to do
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each finding in a
.BR booto (1)
scan carries a rule ID, a severity and the file or sector it was found in.
This page lists every rule ID by engine.
.PP
Severities, lowest to highest:
.BR INFO ,
.BR LOW ,
.BR MEDIUM ,
.BR HIGH ,
.BR CRITICAL .
A case report's
.B highest_severity
is the highest one among its findings. Initramfs, preload/persistence and
package findings carry no severity of their own and count as
.BR CRITICAL ,
except a YARA match on an installed program, which keeps its rule's severity,
because each one is direct evidence of tampering.
.PP
A HIGH or CRITICAL finding makes the verdict
.BR THREATS ;
findings that are all MEDIUM or lower make it
.BR SUSPICIOUS .
.B LOW
and
.B MEDIUM
findings also occur on clean systems (custom builds, third-party drivers);
check them against what the owner installed before you call the machine
compromised.
.PP
.B MBR_UNKNOWN
and
.B RULE_UNKNOWN
appear only when a finding was created without an ID; please report them as
bugs.
.SH MBR AND VBR
Run on raw sectors of
.BR \-t " \fIdev\fR."
.TP
.BR MBR_INVALID_BOOT_SIGNATURE " (HIGH)"
Sector 0 does not end with 0x55AA. The MBR was overwritten or wiped. Common
after a wiper; also on blank or GPT-only disks written by unusual tools.
.TP
.BR MBR_CORRUPT_PARTITION_TABLE " (HIGH)"
A partition entry's status byte is neither 0x00 nor 0x80. The table was
edited by hand or by malware.
.TP
.BR MBR_MULTIPLE_ACTIVE_PARTITIONS " (MEDIUM)"
More than one partition is marked bootable. Legal for some old tools, but
also a way to redirect booting.
.TP
.BR MBR_GAP_ANOMALOUS_PAYLOAD " (HIGH)"
The sectors between the MBR and the first partition hold data that is neither
zero nor a known GRUB or Syslinux stage. Bootkits hide their second stage
here.
.TP
.BR MBR_BOOTKIT_ROVNIX " (CRITICAL)"
Rovnix/Carberp boot code that jumps to hidden sectors before the partition
table is read.
.TP
.BR MBR_BOOTKIT_TDL4_MEBROOT " (CRITICAL)"
TDL4 or Sinowal (Mebroot): the INT 13h disk handler is rewired to load the
rootkit from reserved sectors.
.TP
.BR MBR_BOOTKIT_STONED " (CRITICAL)"
Classic Stoned-family bootkit: hooks INT 13h and moves the original boot
sector to high memory.
.TP
.BR MBR_WIPER_RANSOMWARE " (CRITICAL)"
HermeticWiper-style destructive MBR or ransomware screen in place of the boot
loader.
.TP
.BR MBR_HEURISTIC_DISK_WRITE " (HIGH)"
Unrecognised boot code calls the BIOS disk-write service. Normal boot loaders
only read. Checked only when the boot loader is not a known one.
.TP
.BR VBR_INVALID_SIGNATURE " (HIGH)"
A partition boot record has a Microsoft OEM ID but no 0x55AA signature.
.TP
.BR VBR_CORRUPTED_BPB " (HIGH)"
A partition boot record has impossible disk geometry in its BIOS parameter
block, a sign of a VBR hook or tampering.
.TP
.BR VBR_BOOTKIT_GAPZ " (CRITICAL)"
GAPZ/Cidox code in a partition boot record that takes control before the
Windows kernel starts.
.SH KERNEL MODULES
Run on every
.I .ko
(also
.IR .ko.gz ,
.IR .ko.xz ,
.IR .ko.zst )
under the target's
.IR /lib/modules .
The
.B LKM_*
signature rules match symbol names the module imports or defines.
.TP
.BR LKM_UNSIGNED " (HIGH; MEDIUM under updates/dkms/)"
The module has no signature although the kernel's own modules are signed: it
was added after the system was installed. A module that ends in the
signature marker but has no signature block before it counts as unsigned.
The signature itself is not verified against the kernel's keys. DKMS drivers (NVIDIA, VirtualBox,
ZFS) also show up here; compare with what the owner installed.
.TP
.BR LKM_MISSING_LICENSE " (LOW)"
The module declares no license. Rootkits often skip it; so do some
home-built modules.
.TP
.BR LKM_HOOK_SYS_CALL_TABLE " (CRITICAL)"
References
.BR sys_call_table ,
the table rootkits patch to hook system calls.
.TP
.BR LKM_KALLSYMS_LOOKUP " (MEDIUM)"
Resolves unexported kernel symbols at run time, the first step of most
syscall hooks. Some legitimate tracing modules do this too.
.TP
.BR LKM_DIAMORPHINE_CORE " (CRITICAL)"
Diamorphine rootkit symbols.
.TP
.BR LKM_HOOK_KILL " (CRITICAL)"
Hooks
.B kill
to accept magic signals, for example signal 64 to grant root.
.TP
.BR LKM_HOOK_GETDENTS ", " LKM_HOOK_GETDENTS64 " (CRITICAL)"
Hooks directory listing to hide files and directories from
.BR ls (1).
.TP
.BR LKM_DKOM_HIDE_MODULE " (CRITICAL)"
Unlinks itself from the module list so
.BR lsmod (8)
does not show it.
.TP
.BR LKM_DKOM_HIDE_PROC " (CRITICAL)"
Hides processes from
.IR /proc .
.TP
.BR LKM_FTRACE_HOOK_INSTALL " (CRITICAL)"
Uses ftrace to redirect kernel functions.
.TP
.BR LKM_FTRACE_HOOK_REMOVE " (HIGH)"
Ftrace hook removal routine, found with the hook above.
.TP
.BR LKM_KHOOK_ENGINE " (CRITICAL)"
Khook inline function hooking framework.
.TP
.BR LKM_REPTILE_HOOK " (CRITICAL)"
Reptile rootkit file and network hooks.
.TP
.BR LKM_SUTERUSU_SIGNATURE " (CRITICAL)"
Suterusu rootkit framework.
.TP
.BR LKM_NET_PORT_HIDER " (CRITICAL)"
Hooks the TCP or UDP socket listing to hide ports from
.BR ss (8)
and
.BR netstat (8).
.SH UEFI
Run on every EFI executable on the EFI System Partition.
.TP
.BR UEFI_DBX_REVOCATION_MATCH " (per entry)"
The file's Authenticode hash is in Microsoft's Secure Boot revocation list:
a known bootkit or a known-vulnerable loader, such as the Windows boot
manager BlackLotus downgrades to.
.TP
.BR UEFI_AUTHENTICODE_HASH_MISMATCH " (CRITICAL)"
The signature is intact but the file was changed after signing: a patched
loader.
.TP
.BR UEFI_MISSING_AUTHENTICODE " (HIGH for BOOT*.EFI and shim; MEDIUM otherwise)"
The file is not signed. Fine only if Secure Boot is off and the owner built
it. memtest86+, systemd-boot and rEFInd usually ship unsigned.
.TP
.BR UEFI_AUTHENTICODE_INVALID " (HIGH)"
The signature table is malformed or its PKCS#7 signature does not verify.
.TP
.BR UEFI_FIRST_STAGE_NOT_MICROSOFT " (HIGH)"
A first-stage loader (a file named boot* or shim*) carries a valid signature
that does not chain to a Microsoft root. The firmware of a stock machine
trusts only Microsoft there. Expected only if the owner enrolled their own
Secure Boot keys (sbctl, a self-signed systemd-boot). GRUB and kernels are
signed by the distro and are judged by
.B UEFI_LOADER_NOT_SHIM_VENDOR
instead.
.TP
.BR UEFI_LOADER_NOT_SHIM_VENDOR " (MEDIUM)"
A later-stage loader (GRUB, MokManager, a kernel) sits beside a shim and has
a valid signature that does not chain to the distro CA built into that shim
(its
.B .vendor_cert
section). Shim refuses such a loader unless the owner enrolled its key as a
MOK, so it is a replaced GRUB or a self-signed build. Loaders with no shim in
their folder are not checked this way.
.TP
.BR UEFI_SBAT_REVOKED " (MEDIUM)"
The loader's SBAT generation is below the current revocation level: a
revoked, known-vulnerable shim or GRUB.
.TP
.BR UEFI_PACKED_CODE_SECTION " (MEDIUM)"
The code section is near-random (compressed or encrypted), typical of packed
bootkit loaders.
.TP
.BR UEFI_NO_NX_COMPAT " (LOW)"
A first-stage loader (BOOT*.EFI, shim*) lacks the NX_COMPAT flag. Older
distro builds lack it too, but Microsoft has signed only NX-compatible
third-party loaders since 2024.
.TP
.BR UEFI_ESP_SYSTEM32 " (HIGH)"
Executables in
.IR ESP:/system32 ,
where BlackLotus stages its vulnerable boot chain.
.TP
.BR UEFI_ESP_FOREIGN_LOADER " (HIGH)"
GRUB or shim inside
.IR EFI/Microsoft ,
the BlackLotus payload layout.
.TP
.BR UEFI_ESP_SHADOW_LOADER " (MEDIUM)"
A copy of a boot loader renamed aside, how a replacement loader chainloads
the original.
.SS YARA rules
Every PE image read by the UEFI and Windows boot checks, every kernel module,
every ELF file inside the initramfs, and every ELF file directly in the
target's program, library and temp folders (/bin, /sbin, /usr/bin, /usr/sbin,
/usr/local, /lib, /lib64, /usr/lib, /usr/lib64, /etc, /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm,
/root; symlinks are not followed, files over 64 MiB are skipped) is matched against
.IR rules/bootkits.yar ,
which must carry a valid Ed25519
.I bootkits.yar.sig
like the other rule files; an unsigned, altered or uncompilable file makes all
five engines fail closed. Each rule needs string metas
.B severity
and
.BR description .
A match on an image with a valid Microsoft signature is not reported.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_EMBEDDED_PE " (MEDIUM)"
A second DOS-stub executable inside a boot image, as droppers and bootkit
installers carry their payload.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_EFIGUARD " (HIGH)"
An image naming EfiGuard, a public tool that disables PatchGuard and driver
signature checks at boot.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_LKM_ROOTKIT_MODINFO " (HIGH)"
A kernel module whose module info (description, author or name) says
"rootkit", as Diamorphine and its forks do.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_LINUX_ROOTKIT_FAMILY " (HIGH)"
An ELF file naming a public Linux rootkit: Diamorphine, Reptile, KoviD, Azazel,
Jynx, BEURK, bdvl or Vlany.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_LKM_SYSCALL_HOOK " (HIGH)"
A kernel module that names the getdents64 and kill syscalls and looks up
hidden kernel symbols (kallsyms_lookup_name or a kprobe), the usual way a
rootkit hides files and processes.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_LKM_NET_HIDE " (HIGH)"
A kernel module that names tcp4_seq_show, the handler behind /proc/net/tcp,
and looks up hidden kernel symbols (kallsyms_lookup_name, a kprobe or an
ftrace hook), the usual way a rootkit hides network connections.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_BPFDOOR " (HIGH)"
An ELF file holding two BPFDoor markers: its /dev/shm/kdmtmpflush,
/var/run/haldrund.pid or /var/run/xinetd.lock files, its fake hald-addon-acpi
process name, or its justtryit and justforfun passwords.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_PUMAKIT " (HIGH)"
An ELF file naming the PUMAKIT rootkit's /usr/share/zov_f staging folder, or
its zarya loader together with the .puma-config file.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_XORDDOS " (HIGH)"
An ELF file holding BB2FA36AAA9541F0, the key XorDDoS encrypts its config
with.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_BOOTKITTY " (CRITICAL)"
A PE or ELF file naming Bootkitty, the first public UEFI bootkit aimed at
Linux.
.PP
The next six rules are Binarly's FwHunt threat rules (CC0) ported to code
and string patterns. They carry no file-header check, so they also match the
SPI flash image that
.B -x
dumps and its inflated LZMA sections.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_BLACKLOTUS " (CRITICAL)"
BlackLotus: its encoded strings (winload.efi path, VbsPolicyDisabled,
BOOTMGR.XSL, OSLOADER.XSL) or its winload trampoline-hook code.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_ESPECTER " (CRITICAL)"
ESPecter: EFI loader code that patches the Windows boot manager's integrity
checks.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_COSMICSTRAND " (CRITICAL)"
CosmicStrand: the HandleProtocol hook it plants in the CSMCORE firmware driver.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_MOONBOUNCE " (CRITICAL)"
MoonBounce: CORE_DXE boot-service hooks together with its search for the
kernel transfer routine.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_LOJAX " (CRITICAL)"
LoJax: a SecDxe driver holding the NTFS driver name, rpcnetp.exe and
autoche.exe, the files it drops onto the Windows disk.
.TP
.BR BOOTO_YARA_MOSAICREGRESSOR " (CRITICAL)"
MosaicRegressor: the SmmInterfaceBase dropper code, or the SmmAccessSub
payload (setupinf.log, IntelUpdate.exe and the Startup folder path).
.SH INITRAMFS
Each initramfs image is unpacked in memory and its scripts are searched.
These findings are reported by title:
.TP
.B Reverse Shell Payload
.I /dev/tcp/
or
.I /dev/udp/
redirection in a boot script.
.TP
.B Interactive Reverse Shell
.B bash \-i
in a boot script.
.TP
.B Netcat Backdoor
.B nc \-e
in a boot script.
.TP
.B FIFO Pipe Shell Backdoor
.B mkfifo /tmp/
in a boot script, the usual reverse-shell pipe.
.TP
.B Remote Payload Fetcher
.B curl
or
.B wget
piped into
.B sh
or
.B bash
during early boot. A download alone is normal (dracut's livenet module).
.TP
.BR "Ramdisk LKM Injection" ", " "Temp LKM Injection"
.B insmod
from
.I /dev/shm
or
.IR /tmp .
.TP
.B Untrusted Kernel Re-execution
.B kexec \-l
during boot: swaps in another kernel.
.PP
A malformed, truncated or unrecognised image is not a finding: it makes the
engine
.B FAILED
and the verdict INCOMPLETE.
.SH PRELOAD AND PERSISTENCE
.TP
.B PRELOAD_ACTIVE_HOOK
.I /etc/ld.so.preload
loads a library into every program: the classic userland rootkit hook.
.TP
.B PRELOAD_ENV_HOOK
.I /etc/environment
sets
.B LD_PRELOAD
for every login.
.TP
.B PRELOAD_SUSPICIOUS_CONF_PATH
An
.I /etc/ld.so.conf.d
entry adds a library directory in a temporary or hidden path.
.TP
.B PERSIST_SYSTEMD_EXEC
A systemd unit runs a program from a temporary or hidden path, or code it
fetched.
.TP
.B PERSIST_CRON
A cron job or boot script does the same.
.TP
.B PERSIST_SHELL_PROFILE
A shell profile starts by running a temporary-path program, fetched code, or
.BR LD_PRELOAD .
.TP
.B PERSIST_SSH_FORCED_COMMAND
An
.I authorized_keys
entry forces a command that runs on every SSH login.
.TP
.B PERSIST_MODPROBE_INSTALL
A modprobe
.B install
rule runs a program whenever a module loads.
.TP
.B PERSIST_UDEV_RUN
A udev rule runs a shell or a temporary-path program as root on device
events (the sedexp technique).
.TP
.BR IOC_JYNX2 ", " IOC_REPTILE ", " IOC_KINSING ", " IOC_EBURY_LIBNS2 ", " IOC_SUCKIT ", " IOC_T0RN ", " IOC_PHALANX2
A file exists at a known install path of that rootkit (Jynx2, Reptile,
Kinsing, Ebury, SucKIT, T0rn, Phalanx2). The paths are in
.IR rootkit_file_iocs.json .
.SS Accounts and logs
Signs that an intruder kept access or covered their tracks.
.TP
.B ACCT_UID0_ALIAS
(HIGH) An account other than
.B root
has UID 0 in
.IR /etc/passwd :
a second root login under a quieter name.
.TP
.B ACCT_EMPTY_PASSWORD
(MEDIUM) An
.I /etc/shadow
entry has an empty password field, so that account logs in with no
password. Locked accounts
.RB ( * ", " ! )
are not reported.
.TP
.B ACCT_SUDO_NOPASSWD_ALL
(MEDIUM) A sudoers line grants every command as root with
.BR "NOPASSWD: ALL" .
Cloud images grant this to their default user; a rule limited to named
commands is not reported.
.TP
.B ACCT_WTMP_ZEROED
(HIGH)
.I /var/log/wtmp
has login records overwritten with zeros. Log wipers (zap, wipe) do this to
erase a login; login, reboot and logrotate never do.
.TP
.B ACCT_HISTORY_DEVNULL
(MEDIUM) A
.IR .bash_history ,
.I .zsh_history
or
.I .ash_history
in
.I /root
or a home folder is a symlink to
.IR /dev/null ,
so the commands typed there are never kept.
.SH PACKAGE INTEGRITY
Packaged files are hashed and compared with the package database. Each
finding names the file, its package and the reason:
.TP
.B content differs from package
The file was changed after install, for example a trojaned
.BR sshd (8).
A config file the admin edited can also show up here.
.TP
.B replaced by a symlink
.TP
.B no longer a regular file
.PP
Missing and unreadable files are counted in the log but are not findings.
.SH WINDOWS BOOT CHAIN
Runs when the target root holds
.IR Windows/System32/ntoskrnl.exe ;
the Linux engines skip such a root. NTFS is mounted read-only with
.BR ntfs3 .
A locked BitLocker volume cannot be read: it is listed as a target, every engine that needs the root
reports "BitLocker volume: locked, not scanned", and the verdict is INCOMPLETE, never CLEAN.
Unlock it read-only with F3 in the TUI, using its password or 48-digit recovery key; the NTFS
volume inside is then scanned like any other Windows root.
The embedded Authenticode signature of
.BR winload ,
.BR winresume ,
.BR ntoskrnl.exe ,
.B hal.dll
and
.B ci.dll
is checked against Microsoft's roots. Each finding names the file:
.TP
.BR "was changed after it was signed" " (CRITICAL)"
The signature is intact but the file's hash no longer matches it: a patched
loader or kernel, as FinSpy and ESPecter do.
.TP
.BR "is signed, but not by Microsoft" " (CRITICAL)"
A core boot file signed by someone else is a replacement.
.TP
.BR "has a signature that does not verify" " (HIGH)"
.TP
.BR "has no embedded signature" " (HIGH)"
Core boot files always carry one.
.TP
.BR "is not a valid PE image" " (HIGH)"
.PP
Every
.I .sys
file in
.I System32/drivers
is checked the same way. A tampered or invalid driver is a finding; a driver
without an embedded signature (Windows signs many through catalog files) or
signed by a third party is only counted in the engine's note.
.PP
The SYSTEM hive
.RI ( Windows/System32/config/SYSTEM )
lists the boot-start services
.RB ( Start " = 0)"
of the current control set. Each one's
.B ImagePath
outside
.I System32/drivers
is checked the same way. A path with
.B ..
or through a symlink is not followed. Changes still in the hive's transaction logs are not seen.
.SS Persistence and ransomware
Autostart places and ransomware traces on the Windows system volume, read
from the SOFTWARE and SYSTEM hives, each NTUSER.DAT, and the Startup folders.
.TP
.B WIN_WINLOGON_SHELL
(HIGH) The Winlogon
.B Shell
value starts something besides
.IR explorer.exe ,
so it runs at every logon.
.TP
.B WIN_WINLOGON_USERINIT
(HIGH) The Winlogon
.B Userinit
value lists a program after
.IR userinit.exe .
.TP
.B WIN_IFEO_ACCESSIBILITY
(CRITICAL) An Image File Execution Options
.B Debugger
is set for an accessibility program
.RI ( sethc.exe ", " utilman.exe ", " osk.exe ", ...),"
which then opens a shell at the logon screen without a password.
.TP
.B WIN_IFEO_DEBUGGER
(MEDIUM) An Image File Execution Options
.B Debugger
is set for another program. Developers set this; malware uses it to start
itself instead of the program.
.TP
.B WIN_APPINIT_DLLS
(HIGH)
.B LoadAppInit_DLLs
is on and
.B AppInit_DLLs
names a DLL, which Windows loads into every GUI process.
.TP
.B WIN_RUN_KEY
(MEDIUM) A machine or user
.B Run
or
.B RunOnce
value starts a script host (PowerShell, mshta, wscript, cscript,
.BR "cmd /c" )
or a program in a folder any user can write
.RI ( Users\ePublic ", " Temp ", " $Recycle.Bin ", " PerfLogs ).
Ordinary programs from
.I Program Files
are not reported.
.TP
.B WIN_SERVICE_COMMAND
(HIGH) An automatic or boot service
.RB ( Start " 0, 1 or 2)"
has an
.B ImagePath
that runs a script host or a program from a user-writable folder.
.TP
.B WIN_ACCESSIBILITY_SWAP
(CRITICAL) An accessibility program in System32 has the same SHA-256 as
.IR cmd.exe ,
.I powershell.exe
or
.IR taskmgr.exe :
the sticky keys backdoor.
.TP
.B WIN_STARTUP_FILE
(MEDIUM) A Startup folder holds a program or script
.RI ( .exe ", " .bat ", " .vbs ", " .ps1 ", " .hta ", ...)"
that runs at logon. Shortcuts
.RI ( .lnk )
are normal there and not reported.
.TP
.B WIN_RANSOM_ENCRYPTED
(HIGH) At least 10 documents in one folder (Desktop, Documents, Pictures,
Downloads, or the volume root) are renamed to
.IR <name>.<document type>.<new extension> ,
as ransomware does. Backup and download extensions
.RI ( .bak ", " .part ", " .tmp ", ...)"
are not counted.
.TP
.B WIN_RANSOM_NOTE
(HIGH) A text or HTML file named like a ransom note (decrypt, restore,
how_to, readme, ...) names a Tor
.I .onion
site.
.TP
.B WIN_RANSOM_RECOVERY_OFF
(MEDIUM) The BCD store has automatic recovery off
.RB ( "bcdedit recoveryenabled No" ).
Ransomware does this before encrypting; some administrators do it too.
.TP
.B WIN_EVTX_LOG_CLEARED
(MEDIUM) The Security log holds event 1102 or the System log event 104:
someone cleared it, as attackers do to hide a logon or a service install.
Administrators also clear logs; check who and when in Event Viewer.
.TP
.B WIN_EVTX_LOG_MISSING
(MEDIUM) The
.I winevt/Logs
folder exists but
.I Security.evtx
is missing, unreadable or holds no events.
.SS Boot configuration (BCD)
When the ESP was found, its
.I EFI/Microsoft/Boot/BCD
store is read. Each finding names the store:
.TP
.BR "BCD: testsigning is on" " (HIGH)"
Drivers load without a trusted signature.
.TP
.BR "BCD: nointegritychecks is on" " (HIGH)"
.TP
.BR "BCD: the boot debugger is on" " (MEDIUM)"
.TP
.BR "BCD: the kernel debugger is on" " (MEDIUM)"
.TP
.BR "BCD: Windows loads <path>, not winload" " (CRITICAL)"
A Windows loader entry starts something other than
.IR \eWindows\esystem32\ewinload.efi .
.TP
.BR "BCD store is not a valid registry hive" " (HIGH)"
.SS Measured boot (report only)
When the ESP was found on the target's disk, the Authenticode hash of
.I EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi
is looked up among the EFI applications in the newest boot log Windows saved under
.IR Windows/Logs/MeasuredBoot .
"is NOT what the last Windows boot ran" means the boot manager changed after that boot:
a Windows update does this, and so does a bootkit that swaps it.
The line is printed as
.B Measured boot
and never changes the verdict. The live TPM log is not used: it describes Booto's own boot.
.SH FIRMWARE AUDIT
Runs on the machine Booto booted on, in device mode. Each check is
.BR ok ,
.B issue
or
.BR unknown ;
none changes the verdict.
.TP
.B Secure Boot on
.TP
.B Setup mode off
In setup mode anyone can enroll keys.
.TP
.B Platform key
A platform key is enrolled and well formed.
.TP
.B No test keys (PKfail)
No key marked "DO NOT TRUST" or "DO NOT SHIP" (vendor test keys) in PK, KEK
or db.
.TP
.B Revocation list (dbx)
The dbx is not empty and holds every x64 hash in Microsoft's latest dbx list
(the built-in rules). A missing hash means the dbx is out of date: update it
with fwupdmgr or Windows Update.
.TP
.B 2023 Microsoft CA in db
If db trusts Microsoft at all, it holds one of the 2023 Microsoft UEFI CAs.
The 2011 CAs expire in 2026; a db without the 2023 ones cannot boot newly
signed Windows or shim builds. Passes when db holds only the owner's keys.
.TP
.B Shim MOK keys known
Every machine-owner key belongs to a known distribution; binary hashes
trusted without a signature are reported.
.TP
.B No pending BootNext
No one-time boot entry is waiting to run.
.TP
.B Boot-time variables hidden
Every variable efivarfs lists has the RUNTIME_ACCESS attribute. A boot-time
only variable still readable after boot means the firmware leaks pre-OS state
to the OS (fwupd Uefi.BootserviceVars).
.TP
.B IOMMU described (DMAR/IVRS)
The firmware describes an IOMMU, so DMA attacks can be blocked. Needs root.
.TP
.B Thunderbolt DMA protection
.TP
.BR "SMM locked" ", " "SMRAM range protected" ", " "BIOS write protection"
Chipset registers that stop code from writing firmware or SMM memory. Only
read on confirmed chipsets; others report unknown. On AMD, BIOS write
protection is the ccp driver's
.I rom_armor_enforced
(ROM Armor).
.TP
.B Boot measurements (TPM)
The TPM event log is read and the firmware measurements (PCR 0 to 7) are
replayed, so changes to the boot chain can be detected.
.TP
.B Intel ME normal mode
The Intel CSME status (HFSTS1, read from the mei driver) must not report
manufacturing mode, a security override or debug mode: each leaves the ME
flash region or fuses writable. Intel only.
.TP
.B Boot Guard verified boot
HFSTS6 must show Boot Guard verifying the firmware and its fuses locked, so
modified firmware cannot boot. Read for CSME 11 to 17 only; other versions
report unknown. On AMD, Platform Secure Boot must be fused on (ccp
.I fused_part
is 1).
.TP
.B CPU debug interface locked
On Intel CPUs with silicon debug (SDBG), IA32_DEBUG_INTERFACE must be off and
locked, or a USB debug probe (DCI) can take over the CPU. On AMD, the ccp
driver's
.I debug_lock_on
must be 1.
.TP
.B SPI flash config locked
Intel only: the SPI controller's HSFS.FLOCKDN bit must be set, so the OS cannot
lift the flash protected ranges until reset. Read from BAR0 of 00:1f.5
(mapped read-only); needs root and no kernel lockdown, else unknown.
.TP
.BR "Flash replay protection" ", " "Firmware rollback protection"
AMD only: the ccp driver's
.I rpmc_production_enabled
and
.I anti_rollback_status
must be 1, so an old flash image cannot be replayed and older firmware cannot
be flashed. Intel reports neither to the OS (N/A).
.PP
With
.BR \-x ,
UEFI variables over 4 KiB with near-random contents are reported as possible
hidden code, and the SPI flash is dumped read-only with
.BR flashrom (8)
for offline analysis. The image is walked: LZMA sections are inflated by the
sandboxed xz, and the image and each section are matched against the YARA
rules above. On Intel, the flash descriptor's BIOS master entry (FLMSTR1) is
read: a descriptor the host CPU may rewrite lets malware unlock the ME region
(fwupd Spi.Descriptor). IFD v1 and v2 are recognised by their read clock;
other layouts are not reported.
.SH SEE ALSO
